<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Tech-Free or Tech Bound? – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 5px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Tech-Free or Tech Bound?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a diary entry about a significant event. Interactive: Collaborate in writing, with guidance, on a shared text for a group project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a diary entry describing a day without using a phone (e.g., "Today, I did not use my phone."). Can collaborate with classmates to write a list of activities for a tech-free day (e.g., "I can read a book and play outside."). Can write a short paragraph reflecting on an experience without technology (e.g., "I have spent more time with my friends since I stopped using my computer all day long.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 10px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of people enjoying outdoor activities (e.g., reading a book in a park, playing a board game, talking face-to-face) and another picture of people engrossed in their digital devices (e.g., looking at cellphones, using a computer). Students are asked to quickly jot down three words or short phrases that describe each picture on a small whiteboard or a piece of paper. This activates prior knowledge related to "Tech-Free or Tech Bound?" and the associated vocabulary (e.g., <i>relaxed, focused, distracted, social media, interact</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple reflective sentence comparing the two pictures, for instance: "I think being in nature is <i>more relaxing</i> than using a <i>device</i> for a long time." or "We <i>could</i> spend time outside and <i>interact</i> with friends." The teacher highlights the use of comparative adjectives and modals in their sentence. Students then share some of their written phrases or sentences with a partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'more relaxing' talking about one thing or comparing two things?" (Expected answer: "Comparing two things.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'We <i>could</i> spend time outside,' does it mean we *must* or we *have the option*?" (Expected answer: "We have the option / It's a suggestion.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short diary entry written by a teenager reflecting on a "tech-free" day. The text is projected onto the board. It includes target grammar (modals, comparatives, present perfect with 'since' and 'for') and vocabulary (<i>cellphone, social media, disconnect, interact, helpful, meaningful, focused, relaxed, benefits</i>). </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example text:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic; border-left: 3px solid #1a5276; padding-left: 10px;">"Dear Diary,<br>Today was different. I decided to <i>disconnect</i> from my <i>cellphone</i> for the whole day! It was a bit challenging at first. I usually check <i>social media</i> often. But I <i>have spent</i> more time with my family <i>since</i> this morning. We played board games and went for a walk. It was <i>more meaningful</i> than just scrolling through feeds. I think this experience was really <i>helpful</i>. I feel more <i>relaxed</i> and <i>focused</i>. Maybe I <i>should</i> try to <i>reduce</i> my screen time regularly. The <i>benefits</i> are clear!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand and extract specific information from a short reflective text about personal experiences without technology.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of this diary entry?" (Expected answer: "It's about a day without a cellphone and how it felt.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read the entry and determine if statements are true or false (e.g., "The writer enjoyed using social media all day." - False; "The writer felt more relaxed after disconnecting." - True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match words from the text (e.g., "disconnect", "meaningful") with their simple definitions or synonyms.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using modals ("can," "could," "should"), comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"), and present perfect with "since" and "for." They will integrate target vocabulary (<i>technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused, often, usually, actively</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters or cloze sentences on a worksheet. Students complete them using the correct grammar structure and vocabulary.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You _________ (modal of suggestion) try to _________ (verb) your screen time." (e.g., <i>should, reduce</i>)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Talking face-to-face is _________ (comparative) than using a _________ (noun)." (e.g., <i>more meaningful, device</i>)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I _________ (present perfect verb) more focused _________ (time marker) I stopped using _________ (noun)." (e.g., <i>have been, since, social media</i>)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher guides students to actively listen for and practice the pronunciation of "-ed" endings when reading their completed sentences aloud.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Expansion/Transformation):</b> Students work individually to rewrite simple sentences into more complex ones, incorporating a modal, a comparative, or the present perfect.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Technology is fast." -> "Digital <i>technology</i> makes communication <i>faster</i> and <i>easier</i>."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I don't use my phone much now." -> "I <i>have used</i> my <i>cellphone</i> less <i>for</i> a month."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Go outside." -> "You <i>could</i> go outside and <i>interact</i> with nature."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages production of minimal pairs when students discuss their sentences, like "mouth" (n.) vs. "mouth" (v.), ensuring awareness of /θ/ and /ð/ sounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Writing Exchange):</b> Students form pairs. Each student writes three original sentences about positive or negative aspects of technology or suggestions for tech-free activities, using different target grammar points and vocabulary. They then exchange their sentences with their partner, who checks for grammar, vocabulary use, and clarity, providing constructive feedback for improvement.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative and individual written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part A (Collaborative Writing):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Their task is to collaboratively write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) reflecting on "A Day Without Technology". They need to brainstorm and write a list of 3-5 activities they *could* do on a tech-free day, incorporating verbs like <i>read, play, interact, save</i>, and adjectives like <i>meaningful, relaxed, focused</i>. They then combine these ideas into a shared text, discussing pros and cons and offering suggestions. Each group presents their written paragraph or list to the class, actively discussing positive and negative aspects of technology and proposing solutions for overuse (Sociolinguistic Competence).</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentence: "We <i>could</i> read a book or play outside, which is <i>more fun</i> than using our <i>devices</i>."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part B (Individual Writing - Diary Entry):</b> After the collaborative activity, each student individually writes a short diary entry (5-7 sentences) reflecting on a hypothetical "tech-free" experience. They should describe what they did instead of using <i>technology</i>, how they felt, and what they learned. They must incorporate at least one modal ("can," "could," "should"), one comparative ("easier," "more difficult"), and one present perfect sentence using "since" or "for." They should also use at least three vocabulary words from the list (e.g., <i>disconnect, interact, helpful, relaxed, focused, benefits</i>). Students are encouraged to express their opinions and discuss pros and cons, demonstrating pragmatic competence.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Today, I did not use my <i>cellphone</i>. I <i>have played</i> outside <i>for</i> two hours with my dog. It was <i>easier</i> to feel <i>relaxed</i>. I <i>should</i> do this more often."</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist during collaborative writing and collects individual diary entries for review. A peer assessment checklist is also used for the diary entries.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' collaborative paragraphs/lists and individual diary entries.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric/Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content (Collaborative paragraph):</b> Timely completion of the task (list of activities for a tech-free day / paragraph reflecting on the day). Ideas are relevant to the theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content (Diary entry):</b> Describes a day without technology and feelings/learnings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Grammar:</b> Correct use of modals (can, could, should), comparatives (easier, more difficult), and present perfect (since, for).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate use of key vocabulary (e.g., <i>technology, cellphone, disconnect, interact, helpful, meaningful, relaxed, focused, benefits</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Ideas are clear and organized; sentences flow logically.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration (for group work):</b> Active participation and contribution to the shared text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation (brief check during reading aloud):</b> If students read their work, check for intelligible pronunciation of words with /θ/ and /ð/ sounds and "-ed" endings.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned how to write about ____________________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is ____________________, and it means ____________________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your diary entry or the group paragraph, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can writing about technology help you understand its impact on your life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in engaging students in the writing tasks? Were the instructions clear enough for independent and collaborative writing?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which grammar points (modals, comparatives, present perfect) need further reinforcement in future lessons based on student writing? Which vocabulary words were most challenging for students to integrate?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., sentence frames, word banks, explicit modeling of sentence structures) are needed for the next writing activity, especially for students who struggled?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their experiences and findings from the "Tech-Free or Tech Bound?" theme. They will integrate the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced in writing to mediate information, potentially by creating a poster or a presentation based on their reflections from this writing lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>